[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Pointers and Memory Allocation in Go (Golang)"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Go is a statically typed, compiled language that provides built-in support for pointers and memory management. Unlike languages like C or C++, Go relieves the developer from manual memory deallocation through its powerful garbage collector, but understanding how memory allocation works remains crucial for writing high-performance software."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding Pointers in Go"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"A pointer is a variable that stores the memory address of another value. Instead of passing large data structures by value (which involves copying), passing them by pointer allows functions to modify the original data directly."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"The (&) operator yields the memory address of a variable."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"The (*) operator denotes the pointer's underlying value, known as dereferencing."},{"order":7,"type":"code","text":"package main

import "fmt"

func updateValue(val *int) {
    *val = 42
}

func main() {
    x := 10
    fmt.Println("Before:", x)
    updateValue(&x)
    fmt.Println("After:", x)
}"},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"Memory Allocation: new vs make"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"Go provides two distinct built-in functions for memory allocation: new and make. They serve different purposes and operate on different data types."},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"new(T): Allocates zeroed storage for a new item of type T and returns its address, a value of type *T. It is rarely used directly in idiomatic Go."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"make(T, args): Specifically used for slices, maps, and channels. It initializes the data structure and returns an initialized (not zeroed) value of type T (not *T)."},{"order":12,"type":"code","text":"package main

import "fmt"

func main() {
    // Using new
    p := new(int)
    *p = 100
    fmt.Println("Pointer value:", *p)

    // Using make for slices
    s := make([]int, 5, 10)
    fmt.Println("Slice length and capacity:", len(s), cap(s))
}"},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"Stack vs Heap Allocation"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"Go compilers automatically decide whether a variable should be allocated on the stack or the heap through a process called escape analysis. If a variable's address is returned from a function or passed beyond the function scope, it escapes to the heap."},{"order":15,"type":"code","text":"package main

type User struct {
    ID   int
    Name string
}

func NewUser() *User {
    // u escapes to the heap because its pointer is returned
    u := User{ID: 1, Name: "Alice"}
    return &u
}

func main() {
    usr := NewUser()
    println(usr.Name)
}"},{"order":16,"type":"subheading","text":"Best Practices for Performance"},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Avoid unnecessary pointer usage for small structs, as stack allocation is faster and bypasses garbage collection overhead."},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Pass large structs by pointer to avoid costly memory copying operations."},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use the compiler flag -gcflags="-m" during compilation to inspect escape analysis decisions."}]
